So in 1937, they decided that I should go to Frankfurt, to the Philanthropin, which is a-- a Jewish school, primary and high school. And at that time, they had what is called an English class. There were two English teachers that taught us. And whoever qualified, whoever graduated from there would have been entitled to study in England.

INT: Was-- was that any better than in Bad Kreuznach?

HH: Oh, yes, there were only Jewish pupils. That was a lovely time. I mean, we had practically no contact with non-Jewish people. In those days, there was no contact anymore with Jewish people because the non-Jews didn't want to be seen. And they didn't want to-- to have Jewish friends. That was out of the question.

INT: Did you develop any friendships with people in Frankfurt that you remember?

HH: Yes, but I don't recall any names. There were quite-- that was a mixed class, boys and girls. And we were doing our homework together in the afternoon. But I don't recall any names. I don't remember any of those people anymore. It's all so far back, that it's nearly 60 years ago. Who can-- who can really recall details these days? That is far too difficult.

In fact, I-- I tried to-- to-- to put it at the back of my memory. I tried to forget it. Otherwise, I couldn't live anymore. I couldn't have led a normal life here after the war.

INT: During the pre-war period, were there things that you remember doing, activities you engaged in, as a young boy growing up?

