In Frankfurt, where I was with my uncle and aunt, fortunately they didn't come in. But in Bad Kreuznach, where my mother was alone with my brother, they smashed up everything. And in the end, there was such a pile of broken things, that they refuse people refused to collect it. My mother had to pay somebody to take it away. It was as serious as that. All the beautiful antique figurines that my late father had collected were all smashed up and in-- and broken to pieces.

INT: Was the business affected by Kristallnacht?

HH: Oh, yes, the business, they also-- they smashed up everything. But by 1938 already, there was no business anymore. The non-Jews wouldn't-- weren't allowed to buy from Jews anymore. They wouldn't buy from Jews anymore. And after the death of my father, my mother still tried to-- to continue to run the business, but it was a hopeless case. It-- it just couldn't-- this-- it just couldn't carry on.

Most Jewish businesses changed hands, where [NON-ENGLISH]-- went into-- became Aryan. But our business was actually closed down. It wasn't taken over by anybody.

INT: During Kristallnacht, who were the-- the main protagonists? Who were the people who caused all the damage?

HH: Well, the SA-- the SA and SS, the Nazis.

INT: Were there people in the town who joined in, when the--

HH: Oh, yes--

INT: --chaos was--

HH: --all-- all those-- were all the-- the youngsters and-- and young people that were-- they didn't have the regular work and-- and were jealous of the Jews, they were only too happy to smash up everything and to put fire to the synagogue, which burned down in Bad Kreuznach. And all the synagogues around the country were burning. And everything was destroyed.

