INT: Was it a surprise that the Germans invaded Holland? Was it a surprise?

HH: Well, yes, because Holland tried to be-- to-- to be neutral. They had been neutral during the First World War and managed to stay neutral. And they had every intention to stay neutral. But they had no alternatives. The Germans couldn't attack France directly because of the heavy fortifications. And so they went in a roundabout way, through Holland and Belgium into France. So in no time, they had occupied Western Europe.

And then they caught up with us. And all the Jews had to register. And unfortunately, we were like-- we-- I don't know, we-- we simply-- we simply couldn't escape our fate in due course. Whatever-- whatever we tried to do, we were-- we were Jews, and we were classified as Jews.

