INT: At this stage, were the Nazis rounding up Jews throughout Holland?

HH: Were they not?

INT: Rounding up Jews throughout Holland and Amsterdam?

HH: Oh, yes, oh, yes, and on a daily basis. You were never safe after 8 o'clock, where they would ring the bell. That was now December 1942. And we were then given a room at Merwedeplein 37. That is the same house where Anne Frank lived before they were in hiding. It's-- that-- that particular house is very well known in history. But there she lived with her family before they went into hiding.

So the three boys of us got work sorting metal in a-- in a cold warehouse in the east of Amsterdam. We had to walk for an hour in the morning to get to work. We had to work for eight hours. Then we came back at night, another hour's work-- walk in order to get home. But at least we came to a warm room and a fairly decent meal. You mustn't forget, it was wartime.

Everything was rationed. You didn't have meat as much as you wanted. You had a small quantity. You had a couple of times a week, perhaps, an egg. You had a little bit of cheese. You had a little bit of butter. Everything was rationed. But we managed to all right.

We weren't spoiled. Ever since we had left Germany, we were living, practically, you could call it, in camps, in a camp condition, but not, of course, a concentration camp. But we weren't spoiled and we were quite happy with it. And that's where we were-- we survived the winter 1942 to 1943.

INT: I just want to clarify one thing. At this stage, you were legalized in Amsterdam.

HH: Yes.

INT: The Nazis had no problems with you being in Amsterdam.

