Fortunately, I didn't have to-- to-- we were a chain, and somebody had to bend down to pick up the bricks, and two at a time throw them to the next man. The next man had to turn, throw them to the-- to the next man. And my hands were bleeding after a while from-- from this work.

But then, fortunately, I managed to get a better job. I was building the walls. They-- they were not cemented. They were just the bricks were put on one on top of the other, because that prevented the installations from-- all the installations being damaged in case of a Allied bombardment. Then only the installation that were-- was really hit was destroyed, and-- and the brick walls just fell down.

Well, mid-August we had another bombardment. And at that time, a bomb fell into our camp. And in one of the barracks 30 of our people were killed. And the poor victims of the Allied terror-- [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] as the SS called it-- the poor victims of the Allied terror. In that case, the Allieds had killed them. Otherwise, the Germans would have killed us.

So in the-- in the heat of the summer, we were perspiring, because we weren't used to this kind of work. And then it got autumn. The autumn went over reasonably well.

At that time, I got a infection of the middle ear, and had otitis media. And at the-- I-- but I couldn't stay home. They didn't allow me to-- to-- to be sick. I just got some tablets, and had to bear it. And when I couldn't work hard enough, I got beatings on top of it.

And from that time onwards, my hearing is damaged. I've got a 95% loss of hearing. You see the two hearing aids I am wearing. They help me. But only the last two years, the more modern type of hearing aid, because for many years I was practically deaf, and nothing helped me.

And funny enough, it is a German hearing aid that helps me now.

INT: Heinz, who was in charge at Schwarzheide? Was it the industrialists or the Nazis?

HH: Oh, well, they were in charge-- we had a-- we had people that knew their trade in-- in-- in this complex; in this factory, the works. This was a huge complex, just like Sasol, in Sasolburg and Secunda, with-- with all kinds of machinery, and-- and to produce petrol from coal, because the Germans needed the petrol. Otherwise the war, they couldn't have carried on with the war. That was the most important for them, to have petrol.

And at-- in the first years of the war, they got oil from Romania. But then Romania was invaded by the Russians. They were cut off from that oil. So they were dependent completely on the oil they produced themselves in-- in the German-- in the factories that made petrol from coal.

INT: The--

HH: It is not Sasol who invented that process to make oil from coal. The Germans invented that-- well, either before or during the war.

INT: So conditions were very different being here in Schwarzheide, as compared to your Nazi labor camps which were run directly by the Nazis?

HH: Well, the camp was also run by the SS. Oh, yes, and very much so. And if you got punishment, it was very severe. You-- you had-- you simply had to try and avoid to get beaten up.

I was once beaten up with a rifle so much that I was unconscious. All my bones must have been blue and green. And-- and the-- that Nazi chap came afterwards, and-- and even apologized.

But he said, if he is as furious, he can't help it. Then he must beat. And if he would have killed me, he would even have had two days' leave, and a special [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] allowance. Yeah. So I should be glad that he didn't kill me.

INT: Had you done anything wrong to occasion this?

HH: No, what you done were you just didn't have-- you didn't work fast enough for them. You didn't have-- your-- your spade wasn't full enough with sand or whatever. We were just shoveling or-- or-- or mixing. And that was enough for-- for being hit and killed.

INT: Were there people with you who were killed during this stay?

HH: People were killed. People died of-- of disease, of hunger, of depression. People committed suicide, because they saw no way out.

In late-- in late 1944, we knew that the Allied had invaded Western Europe. We knew that the Russians were conquering back Poland, and part of East-- Eastern Germany.

But it all didn't go fast enough for us. For us, every day counted. I mean we hadn't--

We got less and less food. In the beginning it was-- it was-- it was never sufficient. The hunger was always severe. But the rations got less and less. Even the Germans themselves, in-- in the last few months of the war, were suffering. They didn't have much food.

And-- and obviously, the Jews could die. Who cared for them?

INT: Did you sense urgency amongst the Nazis, and, as far as the-- the war effort on their part was going, at this stage?

HH: How do you mean?

INT: Were they speeding up things? Could you sense that-- that there was a climax being reached outside of the camp, as far as the war efforts that were going on?

HH: Well, in those days, a lot of the very bad SS chaps were leaving for the front. They had to go. They-- they had to-- they had to fight. And we got soldiers-- ordinary soldiers-- to guard us, that were wounded at the Eastern or Western Front. And they were already sick and tired of the war. So they didn't-- they knew the time had come for them, that they would be imprisoned, and we would guard them.

At one stage, I-- I-- I-- I was-- very late. We are not that far yet. That was in-- in March 1945. But I don't want to-- to come to that yet.

The worst winter I ever went through was the winter from 1944 till 1945. In the days before Christmas in 1944, we had temperatures of minus 30 degrees Celsius-- minus 30 degrees Celsius.

You can imagine, with our clothing, we were completely, inadequately equipped. Our feet, our hands were frozen. We couldn't move them. Yet we had to work. Plenty of us had toes amputated, and got two days off sick, and then had to work again.

Fortunately, I managed to keep all my toes. I put a lot of paper around. That is the only thing that keeps warm.

We had wooden shoes in those days, and just rags for socks. And I put paper-- old newspaper-- around. And I don't know how I managed, but I managed to keep all my toes. There is a lot of people who lost a few toes in that severe winter-- particularly the-- December 1944.

INT: You were presumably out there working during snowstorms, and--

HH: Oh, yes. In all kinds of weather. In all kinds of weather.

And at-- when we came in the morning, we were so frozen stiff that we could hardly handle a-- a tool-- a hammer, or a spade, or whatever we were-- we happened to be working with. That's-- that's-- that was-- it-- it was, as I said before, it was slave labor to-- they wanted to kill us. But they wanted to get as much work out of us as possible.

And they-- they thought-- they through they would come with a secret weapon. That's what they told their own people. Never mind we withdraw from the east and from the west, but we will keep the center of Germany. The Allies won't get us. And we will have a secret weapon, and we will get them out again.

INT: And they actually believed this.

HH: They believed it. They believed their Fuhrer. Yeah. They were stupid enough.

Well, of course, there were people who saw the end was near. They were not all that stupid. But a lot of people believed Dr. Goebbels, who was making the propaganda.

INT: The-- the-- the soldiers from the front who were now guarding you--

HH: Yeah.

INT: --they were not Nazi functionaries.

HH: No. And they were very much better. There was one little-- one chap. He wasn't much taller than I was. And he was marching next to me. He sometimes gave me a piece of bread in my hand, because he knew I was glad, with every crumb.

And at one time, he said to me, come on Sunday afternoon-- on Sunday afternoons we didn't work-- and clean my room. And then he gave me the food that he hadn't eaten. So they were-- they were not as bad as the SS people. But the top was still, until the last day, SS.

INT: When were you-- when was your time in Schwarzheide up?

HH: On the 19th of April, 1945, the Russians were about 50 kilometers away from us. But they didn't get on. They-- they had moved so fast into Germany that they had first to-- to consolidate their positions. So we hoped-- we hoped they would come any day. I mean, 50 kilometers-- what is 50 kilometers for modern tanks and modern equipment?

But the Russians had moved so fast into Eastern Germany that they had to consolidate their positions, and to get material and food and everything into their-- to the front. So then it was decided we have to-- we have to be moved into-- to-- to the area that was not yet occupied by the Russians; or, on the western side, by the British and American armies.

So there were at that time, of the 1,000 people that had left Auschwitz in July 1944, there were barely 500 still alive. The others had perished in the meantime, for one reason or another.

And then those that were still able to walk were supposed to walk to Theresienstadt. It was-- it was a-- quite a walk. And those that said they were not able to walk, they were brought to Sachsenhausen. And I was amongst those, because I thought I wasn't able to walk anymore, and everybody said I am a-- a walking skeleton. I can't-- I won't survive the walk to Theresienstadt.

So on the 19th of April, 1944-- 1945-- the camp in Schwarzheide was cleared. Probably about 300 people had to walk towards Theresienstadt-- maybe it was 350. And about 100 to 150 were put into two buses and driven through war-damaged Berlin, to Sachsenhausen.

We saw already then, it can't go on. They-- they are finished. They can't win the war. But how long will it take until the Russians really come and liberate us?

And we were only two days in Sachsenhausen, when the Russians were practically in front of the door. And we had to walk. There was no alternative. There was no transport.

In the meantime, not only we prisoners walked-- the whole of East Germany walked. All the Germans were evacuating their families in horse carts towards the West. So the streets were filled with columns and columns of refugees of evacuees.

We were too weak to walk. Don't ask me how I managed. I don't know myself today how I survived. And I-- I carried along some people who were even weaker than I was. Everybody said, you are mad. You can't walk yourself.

But I-- I couldn't help it. I-- I wanted to save somebody. Because if you couldn't walk on, you got shot at the side of the road, and that was the end. Whoever couldn't walk on was shot. And the bodies were lying left and right.

INT: Did you see people getting shot next to your?

HH: Yes. Yes. Yes. If you had to pass water, or-- or you had diarrhea-- and we all had diarrhea at that stage, because what food did we have? We didn't have food. We were-- we were chewing the grass at the side of the road.

It was just springtime, and-- and the snow had gone. And-- and there's-- and there's this-- a little bit of grass was sprouting at the side of the road. We were chewing that in order to have something in our mouth. There was no question of rations on that death march anymore.

I called it der Todemarsch in my book that I have written at the end of 1945. Later on, it became known all over the world. It's a death march.

It wasn't only-- it wasn't only our group. There were-- all the camps were-- were evacuated, and all had to march, to march, to march. March during the day, and at night we were either sleeping in the-- in a forest, or on a-- on a-- in a-- in a-- what-- what-- where-- where they had animals in-- in the farming, in the areas where they kept animals. There we were sleeping on the straw, on the hay, wherever-- wherever--

INT: And all this time you were surrounded by SS guards?

HH: All the time we were still surrounded by SS guards. As I said, if somebody went out to pass water, or had diarrhea, and he took too long, the SS came with a-- with a revolver, and you had to get up, or you were shot. And that was the end of it.

And then the Red Cross came with wagons, and-- and picked up the bodies. That's all the-- the Red Cross was allowed to do.

And then at one stage during that march, we got a Red Cross parcel. And I was too weak to carry it. So one of the others-- of course, I ate a little bit of what I felt I should eat. We couldn't digest anything anymore. Our bodies, our systems were not accustomed to food anymore.

INT: The parcel-- the parcel was food?

HH: Yeah. And that parcel with food, I only ate a few things, and I couldn't carry it anymore. So somebody walking behind me said, give it to me. I carry it for you.

But I never saw anything of it again-- and that bloke also not anymore. He was gone. So.

INT: You-- you mentioned being in Sachsenhausen for two days.

HH: Yes.

INT: What did you do during those two days?

HH: Nothing at all. That was all-- they were all expecting the camp to be evacuated. There was no work. We were just sleeping there for a couple of nights until we were all forced to march westwards. And this was a-- an experience that I don't wish on my biggest enemy.

INT: During the march, there were men, women, children all together marching.

HH: Yes. Yes. That was-- there was-- as I say, in between there were Germans with their-- with their carts; rich ones who had a-- a couple of horses in front of the car, and a couple of cows at the back. And in the car, whatever position they had salvaged at home, and perhaps a pig that they could slaughter on the way still. So-- so they were well-provided for.

But we didn't see any food. We didn't get anything.

INT: At-- at this stage you were moving west.

HH: We were moving in western direction. For the Allieds-- the-- the Americans and British came from the west to-- to-- into middle Germany; and the Russians from the east. And we were in between.

And on the second-- on the-- on the night of the 1st of-- 1st of May, 1945, we heard the Russian cannons in the-- far away. And we knew they would be very near. And in that night, the SS fled. They disappeared.

And so we stayed-- we were staying in a little town there. I don't-- either it was Wittstock or Wittenberge. I don't quite remember. I must look up in my book.

In my book are all dates and times and everything I-- and all names of those people I was with. Like in Schwarzheide, I was very friendly with a-- with a-- a younger chap. His name was Izzi Kaufman. I wonder what happened to him.

He was sleeping with me in Auschwitz. He, his father, and his brother were sleeping with me in Auschwitz on one bunk, and we got friendly. And his father and brother weren't selected to-- for work. They were probably gassed. And-- definitely gassed, because they weren't selected.

And he came with me to Schwarzheide. And I was friendly with him. We're always sleeping next to each other. And in winter we slept together, because our bodies gave a bit of warmth to each other.

And we each had two blankets-- not wool blankets, mind you. Just quite ordinary cotton blankets. And then we slept together, and we had four blankets, so we were a little bit warmer.

And he-- he-- he should have survived. I'm sure he survived. He was three years younger than I-- I am. And he must have been born in 1926 or '27. I was born '23. And I'm sure he survived as well.

He was amongst those that went to Theresienstadt. Not all that walked to Theresienstadt arrived there. Only some arrived there. Also others were-- the-- others perished on the march on the way.

INT: So you found yourself in this town when the Russians were near you.

HH: Yes, on the 2nd of May, the Russians came through, and we were-- we were free. But we were free. We were too weak to walk. And we-- and the Germans had fled.

So we were living in a house. I-- I don't remember with whom I was there. We just occupied a German house. And-- and-- and we-- they had potatoes. They were farmers. There were potatoes. And there was a bit of bread.

And we got parcels from the Red Cross-- plenty. And we were-- then we were well-provided.

But the problem was, our system couldn't digest fat and good food anymore. A lot of people ate the butter with a spoon, and they died two days later from diarrhea because their system simply couldn't digest the food. And--

INT: You were still in this town, in a small town?

HH: Yes, in that small town. And-- and I knew I had worked together with medical doctors in Schwarzheide. They had said, when we ever get out of this, if we ever get food again, start slowly with a dry plate, and put boiled potatoes, and-- and oatmeal or something like that. Don't start eating heavy things, because our system won't digest it.

And that is what happened. As I say, some people died after the liberation because their systems were simply not able to digest the fatty food.

And I also had terrible diarrhea at the time. I don't know where it came from. Water we had, yes. Water we had to drink. But food we didn't have. But after the liberation, we had a bit of plate and potatoes.

I was a walking skeleton-- skins and bone. My weight, later on, was established as 35 kilo. So you can imagine. Today I am--- I am 70 kilo. Yeah.

INT: Do you have memory of any of the Russian soldiers who liberated you?

HH: The Russian soldiers were-- were simple soldiers. They-- they-- they were uneducated people, and they didn't understand any one of the languages that I spoke-- that means German, English, and-- and Dutch. So there was no way of communicating.

I told-- I told them I've got diarrhea-- diarrhea. Give me something. But they didn't have medicines themselves. So they-- it was a hopeless case.

INT: How long were you in there till?

HH: Only a couple of days in that town.

INT: A couple of days.

HH: After a couple of days came a truck through that place with Canadian prisoners of war. And they-- I asked them, where are you going to? Well, we are going to the west. We are going to the-- to-- to-- to the British-- where the British Army is.

So I say, can I come along? Oh, yes, come along. So they helped me into their truck, and I came along with them.

I had no baggage. I had only the rags that I was wearing.

INT: You were happy to leave the Russians behind?

HH: Oh, yes. Oh, yes. Nobody liked the Russians. That-- in those days either. So whoever could get away got away.

So I came to the British sector in Lüneburg. And there I was put into a hospital. But there was no food either. And I wasn't sick, really. I was just-- I was just weak, and-- and a-- a living-- a living skeleton in-- at that stage, without hair, because in the camps, our hair was-- was removed in all the camps in Auschwitz and Schwarzheide quite regularly. If it grew a little bit, off it went again. I mean, that is-- that happened to all men and women. The girls also didn't have any hair.

INT: And you were in that hospital for a long time.

HH: No, just a matter of a couple of days.

Then I had an experience that I will never forget. I walked around town, and I came to the-- to a British military complex where they offload-- were offloading bread. And they were throwing the bread two at a time, one to the other, like we did the bricks in Schwarzheide. And I begged, please give me some bread.

You think they gave me some bread? They said, go to the burgomaster if you want some bread. They saw me, a-- a walking skeleton, and they wouldn't give me a piece of bread. From that minute onwards, I hated the British.INT: --arranged from the hospital in the British section.

HH: Yes.

CREW: Announce the tape. Tape.

INT: Tape 5. It is the 17th of September, 1995. And I'm interviewing with Heinrich Hesdorffer. Heinz, you were telling me about your stay in the hospital in the British section.

HH: That's right. It was only for a couple of days. I must say, at this stage the Americans were very much better to those they liberated. They gave-- the last bit of food they had they gave to those that they liberated at Bergen-Belsen and Dachau.

But my experience with the British was very bad. They didn't even want to give me a piece of bread, never mind a loaf of bread. And for that I hate them for the rest of my life.

Well, I stayed in that hospital only for a couple of days. And then I don't know who came to me, military people. And where are you coming from? Where do you want to go to?

Well, I came from Holland. Well, it's impossible to go to Holland. Holland hasn't even been completely liberated yet.

And there is no food there. The people are suffering themselves. We get you to Belgium. So they got me on a plane to Brussels with other people. But I don't remember any of those people, neither names nor where they came from.

And we came into a hospital in Brussels. And there we were in a proper bed [CRYING] with white sheets, which we hadn't seen for ages. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] And there, I recovered.

I got good food, healthy foods that I could digest. And I put on weight. And I don't know how long I was there. But after two or three weeks, I was discharged.

I had put on nicely weight. Every day as much as a kilo, if you can believe that. Because the food was right, I could digest it. But I still had diarrheas in those days. And I got, every night before going to sleep, I got a few drops of opium to quieten my digestive tract down.

And then I came into a home that was run by the Dutch government for liberated people from Holland that had come to Belgium. There I was together with mostly young people because the old ones hadn't survived. It was only a few strong younger people who managed to survive all these hardships.

INT: Who occupied Brussels at this stage?

HH: Brussels was liberated long before. Brussels was liberated in August or September 1944 by the American army. So there was, again, enough food. And everything was running smoothly there. There was no problem.

And-- and people visited me from the Jewish organizations. And I gave them the addresses of my family in America and in South Africa. And they wrote or wired them. And they got replies from them. And they were all happy that I had survived. [CRYING]

Anyway-- anyway, from-- from that-- from that home, and that home in-- that was in Laeken, from the Dutch government, and that was closed down a couple of months later. And most people went back to Holland. I decided I would stay in Brussels, because Brussels was better off than in Holland where there was still a shortage of everything.

INT: Did you never consider going back to visit your hometown in Bad Kreuznach?

HH: Not at this stage, I wouldn't have touched Germany, not with a barge pole. I wouldn't have wanted to have anything to do with any German anymore. Today I'm thinking differently.

But at that stage, that was completely taboo, I would say. Because there was nobody there to look for, in any case. I realized my mother and my brother wouldn't be alive anymore. After having gone through Auschwitz, and through-- through what I have gone through, I knew they weren't alive anymore.

It was impossible to survive from 1942 to 1945. That was-- it would have been a miracle. I was lucky that I-- that I was only really in the extermination camp in 1944 and-- and managed to-- to-- to survive due to my-- to the slave labor in Schwarzheide.

Then I came to a home [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] run by-- by Jewish organizations. They were also mostly boys, mostly youngsters. And in-- that was in Linkebeek, a suburb of Brussels. And there I found work.

First of all, I was treated-- I was treated by a ear, nose, throat specialist for my ears. My ears were still running at that time. They were still not properly healed. He treated me first three times a week, Monday, Wednesday, and Friday, and later on, twice a week, Tuesdays and Thursdays, all free of charge. They wouldn't have dreamt of charging anything. In fact, it was Dr. Wiener, was the son of the chief rabbi of Brussels.

And my tooth was fixed again, where that had been kicked out by-- by the kapo in Auschwitz, also free of charge. They were only too pleased to-- to help us and to get us on our feet again.

And then I got work in a-- in a factory making handbags so that I earned a little bit of money. And I earned a bit of money on the black market, dealing in-- in all kinds of things. That was usual after the war in-- in the whole of Germany. And the currency was, again, cigarettes, coffee, tea. That was the currency.

And I asked my family in America to send me parcels with-- containing cigarettes, coffee, and tea. And then I could swap in exchange for any foodstuff, anywhere. In fact, I asked them to send those parcels to Holland, because Holland was still short of so much.

I had friends in Holland that had come back from Bergen-Belsen. And they were very active on the black market. And they got the best price for me. And I'm-- I went backward and forward from Brussels to Amsterdam at that time quite frequently.

I spent quite a bit of time-- my first girlfriend I had in that home, in the Dutch home in Laeken in Brussels. She came-- she had also survived Auschwitz and various experiments by the famous Dr. Mengele. And she was about my age and my size. And we looked like brother and sister. Both of us didn't have any hair. We were both wearing glasses. Everybody said, are you brother and sister? We weren't.

And that way, I-- I-- I managed the next couple of years until my TB was completely cured with a lot of milk and-- and good food. It didn't really take long. And-- and my X-rays were normal. And-- and they considered me healthy enough. But as I said, I had diarrhea still for months and months after the liberation, for which I got opium in order to quieten down the-- the digestive tract.

INT: You mentioned at this stage you began writing your memoirs from the war.

HH: Yes, in late 1945, when everything was fresh in my mind, and every date and time, and details, and particulars, and names, and everything was still vividly in front of me, then I wrote it all down. Unfortunately, it is written in German. I wasn't-- in those days, I didn't speak English perfectly. I spoke English, but not perfectly.

So obviously, it naturally came to me that I would write it in German. And there are certain expressions that simply cannot be translated, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] not to suggest that many a time I should translate it. I-- I can't see how that can be done. Maybe a qualified translator can do it, but I don't think I could undertake that work. The only thing I'm prepared to do is retype it on new sheets and have it available for whoever wants to-- is interested in it, or wants to read it or see it.

INT: Did you keep records at all during the war?

HH: No. We didn't have a pen. We didn't have paper. What are you asking for? We had nothing. There was no pen, no paper.

How I remember the dates, it's a miracle, really and truly. We didn't know-- we only knew it was Christmas because we didn't have to work that day. And we got a little bit of better food.

Obviously, on Rosh Hashanah and Yom Kippur, we have to work as hard as ever. Yeah. And if we could get a bit of pork, I would have swallowed it down without hesitation.

INT: So you were-- how long were you in Brussels for?

HH: In Brussels, I was until 1947. In May, 19-- first of all, both my family in America sent me papers, and my family here in South Africa sent me papers. And I got, in one week, a visa for America and a visa for South Africa, in the same week.

Then I thought, where should I go? In America, I was even offered to be able to study free of charge at any university and to study any subject of my choice. But I decided then I am already at that stage, I was already 22 years old, and I had left school so much earlier, in 1938.

So I decided for me to study now, that is too hard and too time consuming. It will take years until I earn a little bit of money. In the meantime, I've got to depend on-- on-- on family and friends to support me. So I didn't like that idea.

So I decided I'd come to South Africa. My cousin here had a factory for leather goods. And that was my trade. And I could work there and help him. And that is what I did. So I arrived here in South Africa on the 1st of June, 1947, in Cape Town on the Klipfontein,which later sank in the Mozambique Channel. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

A couple of days later, I came up to Johannesburg by train. And very soon afterwards, I started work. By that time, I was normally-- a normal weight, and quite healthy, and quite able and capable to do a job.

So I started work. And I start-- soon afterwards, I started traveling, selling the goods that my cousin manufactured. I-- I spoke English reasonably well. And I spoke Dutch, which helped me with Afrikaans.

So I could go out to the Free State and the Transvaal. And I could talk to the people. And they all gave me orders. And I built up a very nice clientele, I should say.

INT: What family of yours was in South Africa at that time?

HH: In South Africa at that time was my cousin, Elsbeth Morgenroth. She was the daughter of my mother's sister, a cousin, a first grade. And she was married to somebody in the leather industry.

But she was married already in Germany. And they had-- they had lived here since 1936. So they were very well-settled here. And I lived with them for some time, until I took a room in Doornfontein, where I-- where I slept. And I was eating anywhere where I could get a meal for two and sixpence. In those days, that was $0.25. You don't get a cup of tea now anymore.

INT: Were you observant of-- of the religion?

HH: Yes, reasonably, but not-- not-- not to the same extent that I was brought up with in Germany, not after what I had been through, not after what I had been through. Because if there would be a God in heaven, He wouldn't have allowed what happened to us.

INT: So did you feel your belief and your faith was affected by the experience you went through?

HH: Oh, yes, most definitely. So I go to shul now on Shabbos morning, or so I'm keeping the holidays, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] honestly, after all I went through, you cannot believe in-- in-- in anything anymore.

INT: Can you-- can you tell me how you met your wife?

HH: Well, in 1940-- in 1950, I started a business on my own in jewelry and fancy goods. I always had liked jewelry. I was always fond of it.

So I started my own business. My-- I didn't interfere with the line that my cousin manufactured. That was small leather goods, like wallets, purses, key cases. I went in for handbags, evening bags, and jewelry. And I started that on my own.

And in 1954, I was naturalized here. I got my first passport. Before that, I never had a proper passport, a German passport that was-- I was declared without nationality by the Germans. Automatically, whoever was Jewish didn't have a nationality anymore. So I had to wait five years until I got my South African papers.

And then I went to Europe in 1954 and did some buying. At that stage, I didn't want to buy anything from Germany. I didn't want to know Germany.

I bought a little bit in England. I bought a little bit in France. But in the last week, everybody said you got to go to a certain place in-- in Germany called Kaufbeuren. That is where the jewelry industry that used to be in Czechoslovakia was established after the war, the jewelry, the well-known [? Gablonz ?] jewelry industry. You've got to go there. You've got to have a look around.

So I went there although I hated to deal with Germans at that time, it was all too fresh in my mind, all too recent. But still, I bought a few things. And in later years, this-- these people became our main suppliers. Because they came out with very modern items, much more saleable than was made in England, and very much cheaper than was made in France.

And in 1955, I met Lotte. We were introduced to each other by friends. And we had-- we found we had the same background and the same experiences, to a certain extent. And we got married very quickly afterwards. [LAUGHS]

INT: You had family, you had children thereafter?

HH: Our son was born on the 16th of December, 1955. He's our only son. He's now nearly 40. He lives in America. He's a medical doctor, specializing in hematology, oncology. He happens to be here today. That is why we did this interview today, so that he may be able to join us later on.

INT: What would you say, effect, did the experiences you had during the war affect the rest of your life following the war?

