INT: But my question was relating to the Allies and the Gentiles, who during the war might have known and could have done certain things. Yet there are people today who say that they sat back and did nothing then and they are wrong. What are your feelings on that?

HH: Well, the Germans were themselves frightened and afraid that they would go to a camp. I mean, after all, in the concentration camps were German non-Jewish people as well. So if they would really have helped us in any way, they were threatened themselves that-- that they would be imprisoned.

And the Allies, they weren't worried about a few million Jews, more or less. Who in the world cares about Jews?

